      'First Market: Exchange Trading',
      'The first market covers the trading of exchange-listed securities on a major stock exchange. Exchanges have specific listing standards (e.g., minimum share price, minimum market capitalization) for companies that wish their stocks to be traded on that exchange. Securities that trade on an exchange are called listed securities.\n\nExchanges are self-regulatory organizations (SROs), which create their own rules and regulations under the oversight of the SEC.\n\nThe largest stock exchange with a physical trading floor is the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE). Over the years, the NYSE has acquired other exchanges such as the American Stock Exchange (AMEX), now renamed the NYSE American. The NYSE is now owned by the Intercontinental Exchange (ICE), which owns many other exchanges around the world.\n\nCQS: Consolidated Quotation Service\n\nCQS: Consolidated Quotation Service CQS provides current price quotes (not actual trade reports) for exchange-listed securities, regardless of the source of the quote.\n\nCQS provides current price quotes (not actual trade reports) for exchange-listed securities, regardless of the source of the quote. This allows for prices to be consolidated across various trading platforms, enabling customers to get the best price available regardless of where a security might be listed or traded.\n\nConsolidated Tape\n\nConsolidated Tape Once a trade occurs in an NYSE exchange-listed security, it is reported to the Consolidated Tape.\n\nOnce a trade occurs in an NYSE exchange-listed security, it is reported to the Consolidated Tape. This enables investors to see the most recent price of a given security.\n\nNYSE Designated Market Makers (DMMs)\n\nA market maker is a firm that centralizes trading of securities. DMMs are wholesalers of securities and only deal with the members of the NYSE (mostly large banks and trading firms). Retail members (firms such as Merrill Lynch, Morgan Stanley, etc.) accept customer orders and go to the exchange floor to execute the trade with the DMM.\n\nEach NYSE-listed security is assigned to a specific DMM that keeps track of outstanding orders and facilitates trades of exchange-listed securities.\n\nTraditionally, the NYSE was an auction market where buyers and sellers bid for securities, and the DMM ran the auction (and could take part as a buyer or seller).\n\nThe Nasdaq Stock Market\n\nThe Nasdaq Stock Market Nasdaq is a negotiated market.\n\nNasdagq is a negotiated market. Rather than having a physical trading floor, market makers will negotiate prices \'over a computer trading network. Unlike the NYSE, where stocks are traded through a single DMM, a Nasdaq stock can have many separate market makers that are electronically posting the prices at which they are willing to buy or sell a given security.\n\nOptions Trading\n\nOptions Trading There are also option exchanges such as the Chicago Board Options Exchange (Cboe), and the Philadelphia Exchange (PHLX).\n\nThere are also option exchanges such as the Chicago Board Options Exchange (Cboe), and the Philadelphia Exchange (PHLX). Options usually do not trade over the counter. The Options Clearing Corporation (OCC) only guarantees standardized options contracts that are created on exchanges.\n\nThe only OTC options are custom derivative contracts, which are not included in this exam.',
