      'Second Market: Over-the-Counter (OTC) Trading',
      'OTC Trading of Unlisted Stocks\n\nMany securities cannot meet an exchange\'s listing requirements. Other issuers may choose not to list on an exchange. Such securities are traded over the counter (OTC).\n\nSome OTC issues are highly speculative, especially OTC stocks.\n\nBond Trading\n\nAlmost all corporate bonds and all money market instruments trade OTC. U.S. government bonds and municipal bonds also trade OTC. The government securities market is loosely regulated by the Federal Reserve through its relationship with the primary U.S. government dealers.\n\nThe municipal securities market is regulated by the Municipal Securities Rulemaking Board (MSRB), which maintains the Electronic Municipal Market Access (EMMA) website, a reporting system for OTC trades in municipal securities.\n\nOTC Markets Group\n\nOTC Markets Group OTC Markets Group displays quotes for stocks not listed on exchanges.\n\nOTC Markets Group displays quotes for stocks not listed on exchanges. Unlike the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) \'or Nasdaq, it does not execute trades and is not a self-regulating organization (SRO). Trades are negotiated dealer to dealer either over the web or by telephone.\n\nOTC Markets Group has three separate markets based on the level of financial disclosure provided by the issuer. These three categories in descending order of quality are the following:\n\nOTCQX (mature companies)\n\n• OTCQX (mature companies) e OTCQB (less-seasoned companies) • OTC Pink Open Market (speculative companies)\n\n• OTCQB (less-seasoned companies)\n\nOTC Pink Open Market (speculative companies) OTC securities are typically thinly traded, volatile, and highly speculative. Many are penny stocks, so called because their share price can be measured in pennies.',
