      '(ArsSecrtesiddyFemt No Inventory Is Held Fonhekeimeneny',
      'pO "me _ To function, markets must be liquid. There must be a lot of willing buyers and sellers at all times. The more buyers and sellers, the more liquid a market is.\n\nThinly traded markets, such as the Over-the-Counter (OTC) Pink Markets, are less liquid and have wider spreads. The New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) is highly liquid and has spreads that are typically only one cent (e.g., Bid $4.25 - Ask $4.26). Traders: Firms will employ individuals to act as traders.\n\nWhen an order comes in, the trader will be responsible for finding the best price available from other broker-dealers or market makers.\n\nNote: Retail customers do not trade directly with market makers. A retail customer will place an order with their broker-dealer firm. The firm will send the customer\'s order to their trading desk, which will then find the best price available to execute the trade.\n\nMost trades will be filled electronically in a matter of seconds, but the customer is rarely directly involved in the buying or selling of a security. That is why the firm will charge a commission or markup/markdown as a fee for their service.',
